WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
I also want to talk to you about how to write basic command line tools and scripts in Python.

00:06.000 --> 00:11.000
We want to know how to accept command line arguments.

00:11.000 --> 00:16.000
It's pretty useful for a script write you want to interact with the user.

00:16.000 --> 00:29.000
We saw this.rgv, so that's one way of accepting input from the user.

00:29.000 --> 00:34.000
A nice trick for sys.rgv.

00:34.000 --> 00:42.000
We can use a slicing to remove the name of the file because we usually don't care about it, right?

00:42.000 --> 00:49.000
So I can say arguments equal and where would I start my slice?

00:49.000 --> 00:53.000
Which is the first value that we care about at what index?

00:54.000 --> 00:58.000
And go to the variant.

00:58.000 --> 01:01.000
We care about the rest of them.

01:01.000 --> 01:09.000
Now if I, oops, I'm sorry, it was arguments sys.rgv.

01:09.000 --> 01:12.000
So I'll comment that out.

01:12.000 --> 01:18.000
Now if I run this again, we'll see that, oh, okay, this is actually useful.

01:18.000 --> 01:20.000
I didn't get any arguments.

01:20.000 --> 01:23.000
And now I can say, you know, 1, 2, 3.

01:23.000 --> 01:25.000
Hello, goodbye.

01:25.000 --> 01:30.000
And it'll only print out the arguments that were actually useful to me.

01:35.000 --> 01:38.000
Make a new example, our new file here.

01:38.000 --> 01:46.000
We're going to call it input example.py.

01:46.000 --> 01:52.000
To get an interact with user input, we can use the built-in input function in Python.

01:52.000 --> 01:57.000
So I'm going to say that name equals call the built-in input function.

01:57.000 --> 02:02.000
And the argument here is what you want the prompt to look like.

02:02.000 --> 02:05.000
So say hello human.

02:05.000 --> 02:09.000
What is your name?

02:09.000 --> 02:13.000
Generally you want to put a space here so that there's a little bit of spacing.

02:13.000 --> 02:20.000
You can even put a colon or something kind of signify that you want some input here.

02:20.000 --> 02:24.000
And then you can say, you know, print out.

02:24.000 --> 02:26.000
Hello.

02:26.000 --> 02:29.000
Name.

02:29.000 --> 02:36.000
Nice to meet you.

02:36.000 --> 02:40.000
Running this from the command palette.

02:40.000 --> 02:44.000
We'll see that my prompt has changed.

02:44.000 --> 02:45.000
It's a block now.

02:45.000 --> 02:47.000
It's waiting for input.

02:47.000 --> 02:52.000
So I can type my name in here and get my response back.

02:52.000 --> 02:59.000
Note that if you just press enter, you'll get back an empty string.

02:59.000 --> 03:04.000
So when you're taking, when you're accepting user input, it's generally a good idea to do

03:04.000 --> 03:08.000
some validation on it or some syntax checking.

